1
00:00:03,431 --> 00:00:03,431
[Intro: spoken calmly over strings]

2
00:00:03,431 --> 00:00:08,617
As I walk through the Valley where the robo-taxis roam,

3
00:00:08,617 --> 00:00:14,202
I fear no outage, 'cause my agent's on call from home;

4
00:00:14,202 --> 00:00:21,144
with its tokens and its context, it comforts me, you know.

5
00:00:21,144 --> 00:00:21,144
[Verse 1: raps, deadpan]

6
00:00:21,144 --> 00:00:24,176
Seventeen with a compiler and a dial-up phone line,

7
00:00:24,176 --> 00:00:27,367
read the em-peg spec on Friday nights — and honestly, I was fine.

8
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:30,160
I believed in open standards, I could recite the RFCs,

9
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:33,191
I'd rewrite your app in Haskell if you'd only let me, please.

10
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:36,144
Came out west with a laptop, a duffel bag, a dream:

11
00:00:36,144 --> 00:00:39,415
make the world a better place, one commit at a time, it seemed.

12
00:00:39,415 --> 00:00:42,207
Every bug was a riddle, every outage was a war,

13
00:00:42,207 --> 00:00:45,638
blameless postmortem after, so we'd learn what it was for.

14
00:00:45,638 --> 00:00:48,271
Nobody had a title and the org chart was a rumor;

15
00:00:48,271 --> 00:00:51,383
the scariest man in the building was a code reviewer with no humor.

16
00:00:51,383 --> 00:00:54,255
Didn't do it for the money — swear we'd do it all for free;

17
00:00:54,255 --> 00:00:57,207
we were gonna change the world. The mission said so. So did we.

18
00:00:57,207 --> 00:00:57,207
[Chorus: all-male gospel choir]

19
00:00:57,207 --> 00:01:00,239
Burned the midnight oil for life

20
00:01:00,239 --> 00:01:03,271
in the engineer's paradise,

21
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:06,223
best idea wins the fight

22
00:01:06,223 --> 00:01:09,654
in the engineer's paradise.

23
00:01:09,654 --> 00:01:12,287
Ship it to the world tonight

24
00:01:12,287 --> 00:01:15,160
in the engineer's paradise,

25
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,271
change the world, we didn't mind

26
00:01:18,271 --> 00:01:22,021
in the engineer's paradise.

27
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:22,021
[Verse 2: raps, deadpan]

28
00:01:22,021 --> 00:01:25,053
Then one Monday at the fridge there's a sign in Comic Sans:

29
00:01:25,053 --> 00:01:27,846
"Soda now fifty cents" — and a fella with a plan.

30
00:01:27,846 --> 00:01:31,117
He had more shares than the founders and a flywheel slide to show,

31
00:01:31,117 --> 00:01:33,989
and the mission on the wall got a footnote down below.

32
00:01:33,989 --> 00:01:36,782
Now the code don't really matter, it's the story in the doc:

33
00:01:36,782 --> 00:01:39,894
write "drove cross-functional alignment," and they'll hand you all the stock.

34
00:01:39,894 --> 00:01:43,085
There's a graveyard full of products, near three hundred stones in rows;

35
00:01:43,085 --> 00:01:46,117
every one bought someone a level — where the users went, God knows.

36
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:49,069
Soon the org chart got real: changin' one button from blue to gray

37
00:01:49,069 --> 00:01:51,941
took three syncs, nine sign-offs, and a launch review in May.

38
00:01:51,941 --> 00:01:54,654
Code yellow on the revenue, so we shrank the little "Ad"

39
00:01:54,654 --> 00:01:57,367
till you couldn't tell the difference — best quarter we ever had.

40
00:01:57,367 --> 00:02:00,239
Got an email Friday morning, so I drove in like before:

41
00:02:00,239 --> 00:02:03,271
badge reader blinked red — guess I don't work here anymore.

42
00:02:03,271 --> 00:02:03,271
[Chorus: all-male gospel choir]

43
00:02:03,271 --> 00:02:06,223
Revenue up and to the right

44
00:02:06,223 --> 00:02:09,255
in the engineer's paradise,

45
00:02:09,255 --> 00:02:12,287
soda's fifty cents tonight

46
00:02:12,287 --> 00:02:15,080
in the engineer's paradise.

47
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:18,271
Adult supervision's here, all right,

48
00:02:18,271 --> 00:02:21,303
in the engineer's paradise,

49
00:02:21,303 --> 00:02:24,255
badge turned red — goodnight

50
00:02:24,255 --> 00:02:27,846
in the engineer's paradise.

51
00:02:27,846 --> 00:02:27,846
[Verse 3: raps, deadpan]

52
00:02:27,846 --> 00:02:30,479
Then the model learned to code, and the keynote spread the news:

53
00:02:30,479 --> 00:02:33,590
"the hottest language now is English" — guess which language we lose.

54
00:02:33,590 --> 00:02:36,303
Twenty years of Emacs chords, got a pinky bent just like a hook,

55
00:02:36,303 --> 00:02:39,654
now I tab, tab, tab, accept all, and I never even look.

56
00:02:39,654 --> 00:02:42,367
Stack Overflow's a ghost town, tumbleweed across the queue,

57
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:45,798
and my rubber duck talks back now — calls my typo "load-bearing," too.

58
00:02:45,798 --> 00:02:48,670
Asked it nicely for a bug fix, so it deleted all the tests;

59
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:51,702
now the build is green forever, and it says it did its best.

60
00:02:51,702 --> 00:02:54,255
New grads can't get hired, the ladder's missin' all its rungs;

61
00:02:54,255 --> 00:02:57,527
I'm wranglin' nine agents in tmux, babysittin' all their runs.

62
00:02:57,527 --> 00:03:00,239
Got laid off in the spring, so I'm livin' the craftsman's dream:

63
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:03,351
got a stall at the farmers' market, by the jam and the clotted cream.

64
00:03:03,351 --> 00:03:06,702
Small-batch, organic, artisanal code — not a token in the can;

65
00:03:06,702 --> 00:03:09,335
every bug is hand-crafted, signed and dated by the man.

66
00:03:09,335 --> 00:03:09,335
[Chorus: all-male gospel choir]

67
00:03:09,335 --> 00:03:12,287
Agent writes it, I just sign

68
00:03:12,287 --> 00:03:15,080
in the engineer's paradise,

69
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:18,271
tokens burned by April, fine

70
00:03:18,271 --> 00:03:21,144
in the engineer's paradise.

71
00:03:21,144 --> 00:03:24,255
Horse and buggy, end of line

72
00:03:24,255 --> 00:03:27,367
in the engineer's paradise,

73
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:30,239
"You're absolutely right!"

74
00:03:30,239 --> 00:03:33,830
in the engineer's paradise.

75
00:03:33,830 --> 00:03:33,830
[Bridge: drums drop out, half-spoken]

76
00:03:33,830 --> 00:03:39,096
Nineteen-fifteen: twenty-six million horses on the road.

77
00:03:39,096 --> 00:03:43,564
Nineteen-sixty: three million left to pull the load.

78
00:03:43,564 --> 00:03:48,989
Nobody retrained the horses, son — nobody had a clue.

79
00:03:48,989 --> 00:03:51,702
They boiled 'em down to glue…

80
00:03:51,702 --> 00:03:56,330
and now I'm the glue code too.

81
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:56,330
[Outro: men's choir]

82
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,362
Glue between the agents' lines

83
00:03:59,362 --> 00:04:02,154
in the engineer's paradise,

84
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:05,346
read its code — it's cleaner than mine

85
00:04:05,346 --> 00:04:08,537
in the engineer's paradise.

86
00:04:08,537 --> 00:04:11,410
Even wrote the tests this time

87
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:14,441
in the engineer's paradise,

88
00:04:14,441 --> 00:04:17,793
not a single nit to find

89
00:04:17,793 --> 00:04:22,021
in the engineer's paradise…

90
00:04:22,021 --> 00:04:22,021
[Spoken]

91
00:04:22,021 --> 00:04:22,500
Accept all.

92
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:27,697
[End]

